# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The arXiv paper the §1 extraction baselines were measured on.
#
# Not part of the spec corpus and no longer redistributed here: it was tracked
# through Phase 4, then purged from history before this repo was published,
# because republishing a third party's paper is not this project's call to make.
# Several tests still measure against it and skip when it is absent, exactly as
# the corpus tests do. To run them, fetch your own copy into docs/:
#
#   https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.14251
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
docs/LightOnOCR-2601.14251v1.pdf
docs/LightOnOCR_*.mhtml

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Derived exports of the corpus documents.
#
# Two kinds, both hand-made in Acrobat and both used as independent references
# for what we should be producing: "Export To > XML 1.0" for what the tag tree
# should look like, and page renderings to PNG for what is actually on the page.
# Acrobat is the reference implementation of the specification this repo reads,
# so a disagreement is evidence about our reader rather than an argument about it.
#
# Ignored for the same reason the PDFs above are. An export is a derivative
# work of a document we are not licensed to redistribute — the XML carries the
# document's whole text, the images subdirectory carries its figures, and a page
# rendering is the page. Same content in another container rather than a fact
# about it. The failure mode to prevent is a well-meaning `git add docs/`.
#
# An allowlist rather than a list of extensions, because the enumeration cannot
# be finished: Acrobat exports to XML, HTML, text, RTF, Word, Excel, PowerPoint,
# CSV, JSON, and several image formats, and a list that names nine of them reads
# as complete while leaving the tenth to be committed by accident. Ignore
# everything under docs/ and re-admit what belongs — prose we wrote, and the ADRs
# beside it. Getting this backwards costs a republished ISO document; getting it
# right costs one line when we add a genuinely new kind of doc.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
docs/**
!docs/**/
!docs/**/*.md

